ng factors  they're all amplifying the accelerating  the problem so  really folks from all these different  disciplines have been making  similar uh warnings about what was  coming next  and unfortunately we're seeing a lot of  those predictions borne out in  really dramatic and tragic ways this  year  with the escalating wildfire crisis  on the ground right now so it's you know  it's  um it's honestly kind of sobering to see  and i really hope this serves as a  wake-up call that there are a lot of  things that we need to be addressing  urgently um and there's a lot of things  we probably need to be thinking about in  a different way when it comes to  wildfire we already knew that climate  change was  a real and growing problem but i think  um it's events like these i think that  make it  more visible certainly more visceral for  i think individual people because it  really is profoundly affecting millions  of lives right now in the western us due  to these fires  yeah and when we talk about individual  people um you know we have some people  watching  today what can people do on an everyday  basis to break this current cycle that  we're on how would you advise someone  who is  you know not able to go outside or even  people who are watching and looking at  these images and want to  stop this or do something to contribute  to hopefully this turning around  well i mean i think it's it's it's it's  challenging because it is difficult i  think as an individual to to  to really get a grasp of the enormity of  what's going on and the kinds of  solutions that might  uh really help deeply address the  problem and really there are a couple  there are several different distinct  problems i think one is the wildfire  problem which is itself sort of composed  of these three different elements that i  that i keep mentioning  um some of which are more tractable than  others obviously from a climate  perspective  um you know the the the good news is  that we  we can still choose to to eventually  stop global warming by bringing our  collective carbon emissions down to  around  net zero um currently we're not still on  that path so  having conversations with your friends  and families and neighbors about  uh the the importance of getting there  and what it means to you  to be experiencing these climate related  disasters that we're seeing right now i  think is really important  getting out there and pressing uh policy  makers  and you know allowing these sorts of  issues to influence who you vote for  there there's a big election coming up  that i think really is an important part  of this because it is difficult for  individual actions by themselves to  scale up to the kind of solution  the really enormous large-scale  solutions that we need to solve this  problem  but one step in the right direction is  having those conversations and really  pressing  uh folks to to to have these  conversations as well  on the wildfire front there are some  things that may actually be a bit more  tractable i think  in the short term and on the individual  level  in in that there there's a lot of  evidence that how  we build our homes and where we build  our homes can very much influence  their likelihood of surviving a wildfire  being exposed to one in the first place  so there are  there's a lot of evidence out there that  the building materials that we use and  the kind of defensible space that you  have around your home for example can  greatly affect  outcomes when it comes to wildfire how  we design our communities at the at the  individual community level even can  affect  how vulnerable they actually are to  fires in the first place  or what happens to them if a fire does  eventually come along  but i think the most interesting aspect  of all of this is  there is a bit of a paradigm shift i  think occurring right now  in the fire community um and how it's  sort of promoting  uh to the general to the broader public  this notion that  that wildfires are not inherently bad um  the kinds of fires we're seeing right  now are bad  i don't think anybody would would  disagree that the fires that kill people  and destroy homes are bad  um and that burn for us at very high  intensity that may  make it impossible for those forests to  regrow as they once were that all those  things are not good those are not the  kinds of fires  that anybody wants to see but on the  other hand um  you know fire at a lower to moderate  intensity that is that is  burning um not near populated areas or  not  actually directly affecting um  structures and  threatening lives may actually play a  really powerful  role in in solving the problem  this notion that that managed fire or  prescribed fire can actually help  is one of the most effective ways on a  large scale of reducing fire risk  it's one of the reasons why the problem  is so bad right now because we excluded  all of those low to moderate intensity  fires in the forest over the 20th  century  and it's also potentially one of the  solutions in a warming climate because  there is so much risk right now that  there is only so much you can do  to protect communities when these  extremely intense  climate amplified fires burned through  an area so one  possible solution there is to embrace  the notion of  more good fire more of this low  intensity fire on the landscape  as an alternative to the continually  suppressing  the larger and more intense fires  maximally and then sort of losing  control anyway  when the weather conditions become too  extreme so a lot of folks have realized  that  more fire on the landscape in a warming  climate in the 21st century  is essentially inevitable but these  destructive mega fires  are not inevitable we we can choose what  kind of fire we want to have  on the landscape and i think if we  choose wisely we may be able to avoid  some of these  fire catastrophes that we're seeing um  in so many places right now yeah i think  as you mentioned it's not there's no  silver bullet solution there's a lot of  things that we can be doing  right yeah that's right and i think it's  i think even even  you know in terms of of ways to advance  the the narrative the dialogue and move  towards solutions i think even  talking about the problem in that way  which is to say that it is complex and  that it has  not just one single cause but many um  interrelated causes and that yes climate  change is part of the problem but also  yes  the legacy of fire suppression is also  part of the problem and yes the  expansion of the  the wildlife urban interface is also a  problem  so addressing all of these in concert in  a more comprehensive way  um certainly not easy um and it's not  cheap but  like climate change more broadly you  know  the solution isn't cheap but the the  costs of doing nothing  are arguably dramatically higher than  than the expensive solution so you  in order to do an honest cost benefit  analysis you also have to look at the  cost  of not intervening and i think it's  becoming clear that the costs of not  intervening  um in a wildfire sense  are similar to the cost of not  intervening more broadly with climate  change which is to say  it's all going to be a lot cheaper and a  lot less miserable for a lot of people  in the long run  if we deal with this up front  proactively rather than trying to  respond to and recover from these ever  increasing disasters  yeah i think i mean i'm i learned a lot  from you daniel and i  i know we want to be mindful of your  time so um  i think we'll leave it there but i  really appreciate your time today and  hope that you can come back  um hopefully we don't see any any  worsening devastation but  hopefully we can have you back on here  in the next few weeks  i would love that thanks daniel thank  you again  all right
 

